From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: TECH: Report - Entropic Cost of Computation

A little while ago this exchange of information took place:
Richard Kennaway:
>  I understood that in theory reversible computers need consume no
>  energy at all, or at least arbitrarily little.
Mike Price:
> They consume no energy in performing computations.  Energy is 
> expended in the creation of blank memory for scribbling the results
> of computations on.
Richard Kennaway:
> Then the limit you are deriving is proportional to the space, not 
> the time of the computation.  A potentially exponential improvement.
Ray Cromwell:
> But there is a trade-off in achieving ``no energy'' usage, which is
> running your computations slowly. I don't care about zero energy
> usage if it takes millions of years to do some computation. So the
> task is to find an acceptable trade off. Speed for energy.

I believe I have the answer as to how to run reversible no-energy
nanocomputations quickly.  Having rechecked the Schneider reference [1]
on Molecular Machines, after much head scratching, it seems there is a
trade off with temperature as well.  My conclusion is based on channel
/machine capacity grounds.  Channel or machine capacity contains a
factor of:

   log2 ( 1 + S/N )  where S = signal/power/driving motive force
                           N = temperature noise

As temperature drops towards zero the information processing (bits/sec)
permitted per operation becomes:

    d W log2(1/T)    where d = molecular degrees of freedom of
                               nanomachine
                           W = bandwidth
                           T = operating absolute temperature

To gain more processing capacity lower the temperature.  Suggests that
the energy requirements of a reversible computer scale as:

    speed * log (1/T)

Still digesting the article and must run....

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

[1] Theory of Molecular Machines, I & II, Thomas D Schneider.
    Journal of Theoretical Biology (1991) 148, pages 83-137.
    Composed of two sections:
       I:  Channel Capacity of Molecular Machines
      II:  Energy Dissipation of Molecular Machines
