From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: TECH: Channel Capacity and Nanotech op speeds

On the subject of eidetic memory:  The experiment was performed by Bela Julesz
in 1970 (no reference yet, I have it as a personal communication to one of my
teachers).  An eidetic woman was asked to look at a picture of 10,000 randomly
distributed white and black dots on one day using one eye.  On the next day she
viewed a second picture with her other eye.  The two pictures were the two
parts of a random dot stereogram (for which Julesz is quite famous) - when
viewed seperately by each eye they create an illusion of depth, the two
pictures are identical except some portion of the pattern in one is moved
slightly to the left or right.  This eidetic woman was able to fuse the two
images after a day, and to see the image concealed there as floating in depth
above the background.  The use of a random dot stereogram makes it very
difficult to say that much compression was going on, although surely complete
accuracy was not necessary.  Also, keep in mind that we look at things using a
limited acute fovea, and scan around an image to get the whole thing.  This
implies that the image could not be "photographed" all at once, but that some
sort of serial reconstruction had to be done.  Pretty impressive, I'd say.

Paul


