Date: Sat, 15 May 93 13:29:01 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Meta: Solomonic Wisdom Needed

(I changed the name of this thread to "A Contention about Contention"
because I like the ring of that double meaning and then to "Solomonic
Wisdom Needed" when I realized what the focus had become. I never did
figure out what the prefix "I-N:" meant. Sorry, Ed.)

Harry Shapiro writes, commenting on Ed Lane's post:

>Ed quoted Tim May's comments about contention on the list. I would
>like to split the difference between discussions that erupt into
>communications full of harsh tone/voice and the legal methods we
>are using to try to prevent such tone/voice. (We do try to prevent
>the use of harsh communications).
>
>Clearly some find both types of contention ill suited for this list.
>Some, like Tim, find satire of other list members or even the list
>itself to be useful communication although it can very quickly lead to
>increased contention.

Since my use of the term "contention" (strife, rivalry) was used, let me
elaborate on my point.

Anyone reading this list (or nearly anyone, if you wish) would notice that
we are not a very collegial bunch, that the modus operandi for many of the
threads here is for someone to make a conjecture, or assert something
stronger, and then for one or more respondents to:

- dismiss it as errant nonsense
- critique one part of the post, losing the overall point
- "one-up" the original poster by adding some trivial detail

Often done, I am sad to say, in a mean-spirited way. Now lest I sound like
some bleeding-heart, put-upon, wimp, let me add that sometimes this
approach is needed. When real errant nonsense is offered as fact, it ought
to be shot down, politely and thoughfully, if possible.

(By the way, I'm not defending my poking of fun at Carol Moore. I've
already said I probably went too far in use of satire and may have provoked
her somewhat into the flames which she was later convicted of using. I have
taken this lesson to heart, I hope.)

Harry goes on to talk about the legal code of the list:

>I don't believe in utopian societies. Their will most likely always be
>contention. I can't write some list code that will "wipe" out
>contention, without raising the legal contention of the list, and
>perhaps lowering the level of discussion. I think this is a point Tim
>often tries to make.

Nor do I believe in utopian societies, either. My contention (another use
of the term!) is that we are spending far too much time on legal codes, on
complicated rules, and on other such legalisms, and not enough time trying
to create the kind of group we would like to see ("sumbanall of us would
like to see" for Bourlandites). Harry is right that I have tried to make
this point a number of times.

>same tone. Likewise, I don't read every message on the list. I don't
>expect anyone to do so except those who are retired, or have wired
>co-processors to their brains.

It is true that I am retired....I hope Harry's not taking a cheap shot at
me!  <"grin," as they say in netspeak> I don't read *every* message on the
list, though I try to at least *look* at each one. Frankly, there seem to
be only about 50 messages a day lately, and many of these are very short
and can be quickly read. Those who don't want to read them all, don't have
to.

But when people take the time to fire a zinger off--a repudiation of some
point, for example--they at least ought to have taken the time to read the
thread leading up to the point they're disagreeing with! 

Too often the Extropians List resembles a very large cocktail party with
know-it-alls dropping in on conversations and making jibes and critiques
and then wandering off to another conversation to do the same. Absent our
ability to create private groups (as is possible at physical parties)
within the context of the List, some forebearance is needed to stop or at
least slow these derailing comments.

(Why not just ignore these comments? Indeed, I try to. And I suspect others
try to ignore *my* comments when I make know-it-all-type comments. But an
interesting dynamic of lists like these is essentially the "cascade"
phenomenon: most posts are descendants of posts which appeared immediately
before them. Hence, threads are very easily diverted or derailed.)

I'm not suggesting any legal code can change this. Attempts to create a
"polite society" and similar societies were one approach--rude folks would
have sanctions applied to them by the market. So far, though, these haven't
gone anywhere. (I agree with the point someone made that all such systems
need champions who will ram them through, who will personally set them up,
nourish them, and maintain them.)

What I'm suggesting is this:

* That we consider why we're on this list. Is it to win arguments or
show-off our knowledge? Is it to talk about Extropian ideas? What is it?

* That we all consider holding back on minor "correction" posts, that we
not focus threads on sophistry and triviality. I especially urge that
one-line dismissals, like "Sounds like urban legend to me!," be avoided. If
a post is worth critiquing, it deserves more rebuttal (and more politeness)
than such a rude dismissal.

* That more people take the time to write essays of the sort many have
written. I much prefer long essays on some interesting topics than zillions
of "net repartee" comments (and to be fair, this List is less plagued by
chit-chat repartee than most other lists). Of course, that I prefer long
essays is not the reason others should write them!


An Alternative Legal Approach

I also believe a changed focus in the way the List is "moderated" is called
for, or should at least be experimented with, in the interests of trying
various PPL systems. Over on the Cypherpunks list, a list much like this
one in some ways (many of the same people) and different in some ways (no
formal rules, no equivalent of the ExI, more of an anarchy), some
interesting contrasts may be found:

* There are no rules about flames, etc. And yet flames are pretty rare.

* The list operator, Eric Hughes, has, on a few occasions stepped in when
flaming or vociferous and pointless debate was occurring, and has declared
a *moratorium* on debate on that topic for a while. No citing of list
rules, no formal charges, nothing except the impartial (but not imperious)
statement that the topic should be dropped and failure to do so would bring
serious consequences. 

This approach deserves more mention. Understand that I had nothing to do
with the de facto legal code of the Cypherpunks list...it just emerged.
It's much like a parent stopping squabbling amongst siblings by simply
stopping it. Call it authoritarianism, if you like, but it works if used
sparingly. Both "sides" in a contentious debate may want the debate to
continue, or may want the last word in, but the actions of the
administrator--and the threat of expulsion--are enough for both sides to
agree to a ceasefire, a cessation of debate.

* This does not mean the list administrator needs to read each and every
posting, though he certainly ought to be reading enough every day to get
the flavor of the debate! All it requires is that he know enough to step in
and say "The debate on fubar is degenerating. Let's table it for a week."
(And the Extropians list could experiment with other variations, such as a
3-person panel of judges who would by a simple decision issue a tabling
decision, and so on. Just so long as it remains simple, so as to keep the
essence of the Solomonic approach I am urging.)

* This approach has only been used a few times on the Cypherpunks list,
most recently when Perry Metzger and I were debating the issue of "Should
we become Suits?" (No, I won't tell you what that means...I don't want the
debate to start up here!) Eric Hughes tabled the debate for 2 weeks...and
this was about 3-4 weeks ago, so it seems to have worked nicely. I don't
know what Perry's reaction was, but mine was relief, relief that some
"higher authority" had simply stopped the whole process.

I'm not sure what all this means from a PPL/anarchocapitalist point of
view. I'm not generally arguing for authoritarianism, or for benevolent
dictaorship, of course. The key factor seems to be that the list manager
exercise restraint in killing topics and be seen as fair, as Solomonic, in
his actions. The private nature of the List means that no basic "rights"
are trampled upon. A tyrannical list manager would of course be a disaster.

(I think our current emphasis on formal charges, on legalistic
interpretation, etc., is just as tyrannical and disastrous in many ways. We
may have put a legal code in place without the social and market structures
that are needed to moderate and dampen the abuses of such formalistic
codes. This point needs more thought, I admit.)

Part of the way such a system seems to work is that it is itself
reputation-based. A judge who takes pride in his "Solomonic" decisions (as
I'm sure Harry would, but my point is a general one) will be unlikely to
see his reputation suffer by handing out biased or flaky decisions. This is
not always the case with some of the schemes invented on this list (such as
stopping other posters by "shooting" them and hence knocking them off the
list for some time...a scheme that fortunately died a while back), which by
virtue of their very formality, invite "players" to game against the rules
to their own advantage. (Like children twisting complicated rules to their
advantage when what is needed is a fair parent who acts with wisdom.)

Note that good anarchists need not retreat in horror at this
authoritarianism: we are agreeing to be bound by the wise decisions of a
wise judge (well, wise in the context of a few and infrequent judgements).
To use a metaphor many of us have used in the past, think of this list as a
*private party*: unruly or abusive guests can and should be told to either
quiet down or leave. Unruly guests do not cite subclauses of the party
"rules" as a legal reason for them to stay--or to use the legalisms to
attack _other_ guests. The host uses his common sense judgement and makes a
decision. "Democracy" is not an issue--the commons problem is solved by the
private nature of the party (or the list).

Note of course that such "parental" behavior is very much still a
privately-produced law situation. (In fact, I usually cite families and
their various justice systems as the prototypical example of PPL in
action.)

(If ExI wants this list to thrive, perhaps because it causes readership of
"Extropy" to increase, or more donations to ExI to come in, then it will of
course not have a judge that is unfair, abusive, or unwise. Sounds good to
me.)

I would like to formally suggest that Harry, or whomever,  be given the
simple authority to simply step in occasionally and stop or table pointless
debates. Just as an experiment. What do you think?

(Of course, if he "abuses" his authority and stops *this* debate.........
<grin, as they say>)

-Tim May

