Date: Sat, 15 May 93 19:58:40 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: TECH: Numerical Speculation

Responding to Robin:
> 
> In this and another post you seem to imply that there is some
> information threshold below which you can't make any numerical or
> probability estimates, even though you are not totally ignorant.  I
> disagree.  You can always assign probabilities; they just aren't very
> good if you don't know much.

I have some confusion about probability theory.  When you are dealing
with statistics about multiple or repeated events, it's pretty clear what
probability means: it can be interpreted as the fraction of the events
which meet some criterion.  About half of all coin flips over a long
period of time will be heads, hence we say that the probability of a
particular coin flip being heads is 50%.

Where I get confused is when dealing with one-time events, or estimates
of the truth of various propositions about the universe.  What is the
probability that Clinton will be re-elected in 1996?  What is the
probability that FTL travel is possible?  Do these questions make sense?

I think they do, but then I have trouble with a follow-on concept: the
idea of a "fuzzy" probability, one which is poorly defined.  Does it make
sense to say that the probability of Clinton's re-election is somewhere
between 10% and 80%?  I can't quite get my mind around this one.  It
seems to me that a scalar probability number is all that is appropriate,
that we are supposed to boil down all the uncertain factors that make us
judge Clinton's election to be more or less likely, and come up with a
single number.

Here is another example, applying it to betting markets.  The IPSM ran
betting markets on candidates' prospects of being elected for the 1992
elections.  Using the prices of the securities representing Clinton and
Bush you could deduce a market estimate of the probablities.  But was
there also a range of probabilities that could be (in principle)
discovered?  Would their be a difference between a market which was
absolutely positive that Clinton had a 50% chance of winning, versus one
which was uncertain and considered Clinton's chances to be in the 10-80%
range I mentioned above?  Does it even make sense to speak of being
certain about a probability of a one-time event?

I ask these questions to help clarify my own thoughts, spawned by
discussion of the Drake equation and Derek's comments about the
information contents of the brain.  Both of these kinds of analysis
involve some uses of probabilities that I am not entirely comfortable
with.

Robin also replied to the "paradox" I described in which there were
two different ways of counting outcomes about which no information was
available regarding their probabilities, leading to inconsistent
estimates of the odds:
> 
> Where is the paradox, with two conflicting but compelling answers?
> Different "rational" people might have different beliefs, depending on
> how well they knew you, and what they think they would do in your
> place.  If someone subsidized it, there *would* be a market odds, and
> I claim that odds would be as good, on average, as any competing
> estimate made using similar information and resources.

I meant to describe a situation where there was no information about what
was likely to happen, hence I need to postulate that the people don't
know me.  Perhaps we should consider the problem to be posed by an alien
being about whose psychology we know nothing.

The point is, all the information we have is that there are two coins in
some state.  Now, with such a simple problem, the question is, how does
a _rational_ person bet in a subsidized market?  I am not satisfied just
to say that we could run the experiment and find out; it seems to me that
we should be able to predict what would happen.  I'm not faulting the
market; no doubt Robin is right that it would do at least as well as any
other mode of analysis.  I'm claiming that the notion of probability is
fundamentally incoherent in this situation, hence whatever the market
reveals, it is not probabilities.

Hal

